From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 05:20:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Guided Story: stop writing storage paths and whole
 storyboards into prompts
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Job #69001 rendered the mother as two visibly different women across three
scenes and returned one character in a shot that asks for three. Three of the
reasons were in this one 60-line file.

  1. `identity reference /objects/4/uploads/<uuid>` went into the prompt as
     text. That is tenant storage — no video model resolves it — so the
     identity direction did nothing while reading as though it worked, and a
     wrong-person render looked like a model quality problem. Identity travels
     as an attached reference; generateVideo already takes `assetIds` and
     providers/atlascloud.ts already turns them into `reference_images`
     with @ImageN naming. This file just has to stop competing with it.
  2. The backdrop direction carried the whole storyboard — "Scene 1: …
     Scene 2: … Scene 3: …", the last truncated mid-sentence at "She...." —
     into every scene, handing the model three shots and asking for one.
     stripStoryboardDump cuts everything from the first scene heading.
  3. The cast was described but never closed, so a scene asking to end on all
     three together could return one person and be, on its own terms, correct.
     It is now stated as an exact set that no one enters or leaves.

Also: role descriptions were personality ("anxious but witty"), which is not
visible, so the model invented a look per scene — appearance now comes from
the reference and the prompt carries only the wardrobe, which is actionable.
A character with no approved outfit used to put the literal word "MISSING"
into the prompt; it now fails with something an operator can act on. The logo
path became a request for logo space, since the path was never openable and
the bare word invites the model to invent one.

`referenceLabels` is the hook for the reference work: pass "@Image1",
"@Image2" in cast order once assetIds really are attached, and each character
is bound to its own reference. Left unset until then, because naming a
reference that was not sent is worse than naming none.

The old test asserted the broken wording verbatim ("preserves the existing
visual prompt wording and order"), so it is replaced rather than extended.

